  <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
    
  <html:span style="font-size: 2em">The category solver, animated</html:span>

    <html:p><html:em>How associativity evaporates.</html:em></html:p>
    <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em">depicting <html:code>solveCat!</html:code> from
       <html:code><![CDATA[ Cubical.Tactics.CategorySolver]]></html:code></html:span></html:p>
  </html:div>

</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="text-align: center"><html:p><html:strong>The goal</html:strong></html:p></html:div>

  <html:p><html:code>solveCat!</html:code> proves equations that hold up to associativity and units.
     Take the associator:</html:p>
  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
  <html:span style="font-size: 1.4em">
    
  <html:span data-id="lp"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lparen ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="f"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op1a"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\star \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="g"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="rp"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\rparen ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op2a"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\star \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="h"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  </html:span>
</html:div>

  <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em">We must prove this equals <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f \star  \lparen  g \star  h\rparen ]]></fr:tex>. In a bare
     category that is the associativity axiom --- a long chain needs quadratically
     many.</html:span></html:p>
</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="text-align: center"><html:p><html:strong>Step 1 --- reflect into the free category</html:strong></html:p></html:div>

  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
  <html:span style="font-size: 1.4em">
    
  <html:span data-id="lp"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lparen ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="up1"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\uparrow ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="f"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op1b"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\star _e\;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="up2"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\uparrow ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="g"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="rp"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\rparen ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op2b"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\star _e\;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="up3"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\uparrow ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="h"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  </html:span>
</html:div>

  <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em"><html:code>solveCat!</html:code> quotes the syntax into
     <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{\mathcal  C}]]></fr:tex> = <html:code><![CDATA[ FreeCat(Cat→Quiver 𝓒)]]></html:code>: each
     atom becomes an edge <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\uparrow  f]]></fr:tex>, each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\star ]]></fr:tex> a formal <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\star _e]]></fr:tex>. The
     brackets remain --- the free category is just syntax.</html:span></html:p>
</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="text-align: center"><html:p><html:strong>Step 2 --- quote into the presheaf category</html:strong></html:p></html:div>

  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
  <html:span style="font-size: 1.4em">
    
  <html:span data-id="lp"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lparen ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y1"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="f"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op1c"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\circ \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y2"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="g"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="rp"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\rparen ]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op2c"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\circ \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y3"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="h"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  </html:span>
</html:div>

  <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em"><html:code>eval</html:code> applies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal  I]]></fr:tex> = <html:code>PseudoYoneda</html:code> into
     <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal  P]]></fr:tex> = <html:code><![CDATA[ PowerCategory(W𝓒.ob) SET]]></html:code>. Each
     morphism becomes a <html:em>function</html:em> on hom-sets --- its CPS form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,f]]></fr:tex>
     --- and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\star _e]]></fr:tex> becomes function composition <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\circ ]]></fr:tex>.</html:span></html:p>
</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="text-align: center"><html:p><html:strong>Step 3 --- the brackets evaporate</html:strong></html:p></html:div>

  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
  <html:span style="font-size: 1.4em">
    
  <html:span data-id="y1"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="f"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op1c"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\circ \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y2"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="g"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op2c"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\circ \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y3"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="h"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  </html:span>
</html:div>

  <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em">Function composition is <html:em>definitionally</html:em> associative:
     <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lparen  p \circ  q\rparen  \circ  r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p \circ  \lparen  q \circ  r\rparen ]]></fr:tex>
     are the same <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lambda  x.\, p\,\lparen  q\,\lparen  r\,x\rparen \rparen ]]></fr:tex>. Both
     bracketings reduce to this one normal form --- so downstairs the equation is
     literally <html:code>refl</html:code>.</html:span></html:p>
</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="text-align: center"><html:p><html:strong>Step 4 --- reflect it back</html:strong></html:p></html:div>

  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
  <html:span style="font-size: 1.4em">
    
  <html:span data-id="y1"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="f"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op1c"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\circ \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y2"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="g"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="op2c"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\;\circ \;]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="y3"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {y}\,]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  <html:span data-id="h"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h]]></fr:tex></html:span>

  </html:span>
</html:div>

  <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em"><html:code>PseudoYoneda</html:code> is faithful (<html:code>isFaithfulPseudoYoneda</html:code>), so the
     <html:code>refl</html:code> downstairs reflects back up to equality in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{\mathcal  C}]]></fr:tex>, and
     <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\llbracket  - \rrbracket _c]]></fr:tex> transports it into <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal  C]]></fr:tex>. The associator
     you needed is recovered --- from <html:code>refl</html:code>.</html:span></html:p>
</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center">
    <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 1.4em">The formal associations were never really there.</html:span></html:p>
    <html:p>In the strict world of functions they <html:em>evaporate</html:em>; the Yoneda embedding
       carries the triviality home.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:span style="font-size: 0.75em">Same trick as <html:em>difference lists</html:em> / Cayley's theorem: represent an
       element by what you compose onto it, and reassociation becomes free.</html:span></html:p>
  </html:div>

</html:section>
</fr:mainmatter>
