  <html:div style="text-align: center">
  <html:span style="font-size: 0.75em">the parentheses slide to re-bracket; <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a,b,c]]></fr:tex> keep their identity</html:span>
</html:div>

</html:section>

  <html:section data-auto-animate="true">
  
  <html:div style="text-align: center"><html:p><html:strong>How</html:strong></html:p></html:div>

  <html:p>Each movable token is <html:code><![CDATA[\anim{id}{...}]]></html:code> with a stable
     id. <html:code><![CDATA[\slideAnim{...}]]></html:code> marks a slide that tweens to its
     neighbour: matched ids glide, unmatched fade in/out.</html:p>
  <html:p>No timeline, no keyframes, no <html:code>run_time</html:code> --- you write two <html:em>states</html:em>
     and the motion is derived. That is the whole engine behind
     <fr:link href="/sss-00E0/" title="The category solver, animated" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00E0/" display-uri="sss-00E0" type="local">the category solver demo</fr:link>.</html:p>
